# Cryptographically authenticated evaluation artifacts.
#
# The Forgewing V2 Phase C accepted pins are sha256 hashes of these files as
# stored in Git (pure LF). Any end-of-line translation on checkout changes the
# bytes on disk, so authentication fails and the bounded measurement aborts.
# That is exactly what happened on Windows: this file was previously UTF-16
# encoded, so Git could not parse it, its rule was inert, and the system-level
# core.autocrlf=true converted these artifacts to CRLF at checkout.
#
# "-text" disables end-of-line conversion in BOTH directions, so disk bytes
# always equal blob bytes regardless of platform or developer git config.
# This is deliberately scoped to the authenticated artifact tree: a repository-
# wide text rule would renormalize ~1094 unrelated working-tree files.
#
# This file must remain UTF-8 (or ASCII) with LF endings, or Git will silently
# ignore every rule in it. The regression test in
# lib/evaluation/forgewing/phaseCArtifactByteStability.test.ts enforces that.

scripts/evaluation/artifacts/**    -text

# Supabase migrations are a canonical-LF byte contract.
#
# Audit (2026-09-08) over all 101 committed migrations found every blob is
# already pure LF, so "text eol=lf" is a provable no-op on committed bytes: no
# historical migration is renormalized or modified by this rule.
#
# "eol=lf" (not "-text") is deliberate. Both keep today's blobs intact, but
# "-text" preserves whatever bytes an author commits, so a migration authored
# on Windows would enter the tree as a CRLF blob and permanently break the LF
# contract. "text eol=lf" normalizes future migrations to LF and forces LF on
# checkout, so disk bytes equal blob bytes on every platform and migration
# SHA-256 values reproduce cross-machine.
#
# This differs from the artifact rule above on purpose: those artifacts are
# hash-pinned and their blobs must be taken exactly as stored, so "-text" is
# correct there. Migrations instead require a canonical LF form.

supabase/migrations/**    text eol=lf
